in the modern world you can enter a bank  deposit money  travel halfway across the planet and  retrieve that money no issues  but could you do this in the past and  with that how did banks work back then  now banking hasn't changed all too much  throughout european history and as such  we're going to focus on the late  medieval early renaissance period for  two main reasons  one renaissance banks operated across  borders in different states with  different laws and two  the catholic church expressly forbid the  act of charging interest on loans a  practice known as usury  which is what we'll begin with so since  a banker couldn't charge interest on a  loan how did they make profit and why  bother loaning money out at all  well banking families like the medicis  weren't fools and they managed to get  around  interest in two ways the first was via  late payment fines  whilst you couldn't charge interest at  say 10 percent you could find someone 10  percent of the total of the loan if they  were late in their payments  if you were to say payback alone on time  you'd get no fines but in return you'd  likely be blacklisted by every banking  family in existence  and so if you ever wanted another loan  again you'd need to pay it back  late the second way they got around  interest was via currency conversion  you'd get out a loan which would be  provided in one currency  and you'd then pay it back in a  different currency at a different time  and thus  at a different exchange rate the bank  would then pocket the difference  the exception to these rules were kings  and the pope who just got the loan for  free because please don't kill or  excommunicate me  now as the process of taking out a loan  suggests she'd be taking out and paying  back money  in different places meaning that these  banks had multiple branches  as the medicis did so how did these  banks confirm details and prevent fraud  between the different branches  why couldn't someone simply forge a note  saying that they have an account and  then run off with the money  well there were several checks and steps  to verify information  as many of you will know banks at this  time were run by families and members of  these families would be sent to the  bank's branches to run them  these branch managers would know each  other quite well and importantly know  each other's handwriting well too  the bank would then use their own  trusted messengers who would travel as  fast as they could to the numerous  branches to update them on account  information  thus it was possible for a person to  deposit money in say florence and  withdraw it in paris  for a fee that is now all of this was  very profitable but this didn't stop  most banks from going bust very quickly  and this was mostly down to warfare  kings needed money for wars and having a  branch in a kingdom meant that you were  obligated to lend money to the king if  they needed it  and this led to kings taking advantage  of banks since they had near absolute  authority they could  and did simply refuse to pay the loan  back in fact kings were often pressured  by their own merchants and lords to do  so  since this would then force a bank to  default and close meaning that it  couldn't collect payments from anyone  else  and so that's an extremely simplified  way of how medieval banking worked  despite the lack of modern technology or  the ability to charge interest banks  were extremely profitable however it was  an extremely risky business two and  families were often taken down by kings  as quickly as they rose up  i hope you enjoyed this episode and  thank you for watching with a special  thanks to my patrons  james bissonette kelly money maker the  pastry section marvin cassell rob  waterhouse moe  aaron the white james castaneda danny  maloney jordan longley marcus arsenault  gustav swann jerry lambton john bailey  spinning three plates  rasheed ali calling castleman david  silverman izzy  coppertone maggie paxkowski winston  cayward lexy schwinn spencer lightfoot  robert wedzill fortunate calf anthony  beckett and skye chappelle
 

